      "title": "Groups of Suffragists",
      "text": "By 1890, the failure to achieve women's suffrage led several women's groups to merge together into the National American Woman Suffrage Association.  National American Woman Suffrage Association (NAWSA), under the leadership of Carrie Chapman Catt, was a moderate organization. The NAWSA undertook campaigns to enfranchise women in individual states, and simultaneously lobbied President Wilson and Congress to pass a woman suffrage Constitutional Amendment. In the 1910s, NAWSA’s membership numbered in the millions.\n\nAlice Paul and Lucy Burns, originally members of the NAWSA, were appointed to the troubled Congressional Committee. Paul and Burns were radicalized by their experiences in England, which included violent confrontations with authorities, jail sentences, hunger strikes, and force-feedings, and they sought to inject these radical ways into the American campaign.  The President of the NAWSA, Carrie Chapman Catt, didn’t believe in the radical tactics that they were using.   This disagreement  led to the formation of the National Women’s Party, a group separate from NAWSA that also endeavored to fight for women’s suffrage.   National Woman’s Party (NWP) was a more militant organization led by Alice Paul and Lucy Burns. The NWP undertook radical actions, including picketing the White House, in order to convince Wilson and Congress to pass a woman suffrage amendment. In 1920, 72 years after the Seneca Falls Convention, the 19th Amendment giving women",
